--- body ---
## Context

Child of 0817. The rung-3 driver runs its scenario on four Menagerie documents
(`--fixture-documents`). The rung is the whole corpus — 114 attachments today,
two more when 0794 lands (not blocked on it).

## Actions

1. Import the full Menagerie package; no truncation.
2. After the run settles, read every attachment's status through
   `Zotero.SDTPackSitter.inspect(id)` (`scheduler.js` status classes; exposed
   in `bootstrap.js`) and assert none is left `queued`: each ends `indexed` or
   `unindexed` (a pack) or `failed` / `outOfScope` (a named reason), recorded
   per attachment. No new sitter-side plumbing.
3. Replace the four-document timeouts with named, documented ones sized for
   the whole corpus (the 900 s index default was tuned for four small files);
   a run that times out says so rather than reading as a sitter failure.
4. Run 0816's integrity check over the run.

## Test

Red first: a payload that leaves one attachment `queued` after settling must
fail the completeness assertion.

## Exit criteria

A recorded whole-Menagerie run with every attachment accounted for and the
integrity check green.

## Plan (raid 2026-09-23)

- **Two truncations**: `--fixture-documents` (default 4) and
  `pick_menagerie_documents()`'s `*.pdf` glob (`bench/fixtures/smoke_library.py:89`).
  The package holds 44 PDF + 2 EPUB of 114; the other 68 are html, txt, xlsx,
  zip, jpg, docx, wikitext, tgz, djvu. Lift both for this rung. **Keep the
  smoke rung's selection unchanged**: it calls the same function, and smallest
  files of any type would hand it HTML with no pack. Add a parameter (e.g.
  `suffixes=None` meaning all) rather than changing the default.
- Expected count from an independent listing of `attachments/`, read at run
  time; never hardcoded.
- Name the import timeout (`--import-timeout`; today an unnamed 180 s in
  `import_with_retry`). Size `--index-timeout` from the one measured rate
  (`verification/SDT-CAPS-0483.md`: ~5 pages/s, one PDF) — about 2 h for the
  572 MB of PDF and EPUB, **derived, not measured**; set a generous default and
  say so in its comment. The first real run records its wall time.
- Completeness in two steps: wait on the sitter `state` until
  `pending` is empty and not busy (the scheduler's own list already reclassifies
  a document it gave up on as `failed-session`, which raw `inspect()` cannot
  see); then sweep every attachment with `Zotero.SDTPackSitter.inspect(id)`
  (extend the `ATTACHMENTS` eval), classify against an embedded copy of
  `SDT_STATUS_CLASSES`, re-poll once any still `queued`, record
  `{id, key, status, class}` for all, fail on any left `queued`.
- Tests: `pick_menagerie_documents` returns every file with suffixes=None and
  PDFs only by default; a guard that the embedded status classes match
  `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` verbatim; the classifier fails on a
  `queued` entry and passes on the other four classes.
- Live red: pin `Zotero.PDFWorker._processingQueue = true` over the debugger
  channel before the last attachment is admitted, so it stays genuinely
  `queued`; no plugin edit.
